      "hints": [
        "AP 1: $a = 15, d = 4 \\implies S_n = \\frac{n}{2}(26 + 4n) = 2n^2 + 13n$.",
        "AP 2: $a = 420, d = -5 \\implies S_n = \\frac{n}{2}(845 - 5n)$.",
        "Equate and solve for $n > 0$: $n = 91$."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Set up the sum of the first $n$ terms for the first arithmetic progression ($a_1 = 15, d_1 = 19 - 15 = 4$):\n$$S_{n, 1} = \\frac{n}{2}\\left(2(15) + (n - 1)4\\right) = \\frac{n}{2}(30 + 4n - 4) = \\frac{n}{2}(4n + 26) = 2n^2 + 13n$$",
        "Set up the sum for the second arithmetic progression ($a_2 = 420, d_2 = 415 - 420 = -5$):\n$$S_{n, 2} = \\frac{n}{2}\\left(2(420) + (n - 1)(-5)\\right) = \\frac{n}{2}(840 - 5n + 5) = \\frac{n}{2}(845 - 5n)$$",
        "Equate the two sums and solve for $n$ ($n \\ne 0$):\n$$2n^2 + 13n = \\frac{n}{2}(845 - 5n) \\implies 4n + 26 = 845 - 5n$$\n$$9n = 845 - 26 = 819 \\implies n = \\frac{819}{9} = 91$$"
      ],
